A.cards("c9s6",[{"id": "g9-6-standard", "h": "9.6 · Evidence clinic", "c": ["c9-6-core"], "src": ["TB9:9.6"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Separate the regional time pattern from local environmental effects.</li><li>Dominance during an era does not necessarily mark first origin.</li><li>Survival depends on needs, conditions and inherited variation.</li><li>A changing resource can affect an entire ecological relationship.</li><li>An observed succession and its explanation are different claims.</li></ul></section><p>Faunal succession is an observed pattern; mechanisms explain that pattern.</p>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Name the event being dated and the observation supporting it. A reconstructed sequence is not an official future-exam key."], "media": []}]);
A.match("e2-c9-standard-6-bank","c9-6-core","Match each complete source-supported observation or description to the appropriate interpretation. A bank term may be reused if needed.",[["Systematic change of fossil assemblages through a section", "Faunal succession"], ["Observed changes explained by differential survival and reproduction", "Natural selection"], ["Long relative stability with geologically rapid change", "Punctuated equilibrium"], ["Loss of the only food source used by an animal", "Ecological vulnerability"]],"Faunal succession is an observed pattern; mechanisms explain that pattern.",["TB9:9.6"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C9S6"], "wordBank": true});
A.mc("e2-c9-standard-6-check","c9-6-core","A fossil disappears from a hypothetical local column where the depositional environment changes. What follows directly?",["*Local conditions or preservation can explain the absence|One local disappearance does not establish extinction throughout the world.", "The upper bed must postdate the species global extinction|The species may persist elsewhere even if the new local environment excludes it.", "The disappearance defines a boundary at every other site|A local environmental change is insufficient to establish a synchronous global boundary.", "The species range begins where its last fossil is found|Last observed occurrence is different from first appearance, and either can be affected by preservation."],"One local disappearance does not establish extinction throughout the world.",["TB9:9.6"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C9S6"], "media": {"kind": "img", "src": "animations/chapter9/source/9.6-p7-Im1.png", "alt": "Original course illustration for section 9.6, PDF page 7", "cap": "Exploring Geology · 9.6 · PDF p7 · unchanged original figure"}, "sourceFigure": true});
