A.mc("e2-c9-standard-14-check","c9-14-core","Groundwater has an isotope age younger than its sedimentary aquifer. Which distinction should guide interpretation?",["*Water entry can postdate formation of the aquifer rock|The water history and the formation of its enclosing rock are different dated events.", "Aquifer deposition began when the dated water entered|The rock can form before the water later enters its pore system.", "The water age measures the time needed to fold the aquifer|A groundwater entry age does not measure the duration of tectonic folding.", "The water age dates crystallization of the original source grains|Source-grain crystallization is distinct from later sediment deposition and groundwater entry."],"The water history and the formation of its enclosing rock are different dated events.",["TB9:9.14"],{"examId": "exam2", "mock": false, "topics": ["E2C9S14"], "media": {"kind": "img", "src": "animations/chapter9/source/9.14-p6-Im1.png", "alt": "Original course illustration for section 9.14, PDF page 6", "cap": "Exploring Geology · 9.14 · PDF p6 · unchanged original figure"}, "sourceFigure": true});
